            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is up&up protein powder any good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is the cheapest protein per gram at Target, roughly $1.24 per 25 g on the 32 oz tub. The trade-off is the label: about 170 calories and 10 g of carbohydrate per 43 g serving, with maltodextrin high on the ingredient list, per the listing. It is fine if price matters most; a name-brand whey gives a cleaner panel for slightly more.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is protein powder cheaper at Target or Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on the day and the size. Target's sale prices and Circle offers sometimes beat Amazon on small tubs, while Amazon usually sells the largest sizes, which have the lowest cost per 25 g. Compare cost per 25 g of protein rather than tub price, and buy from the retailer or the brand's official storefront rather than unknown sellers.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Target Circle discount protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Sometimes. Target Circle offers rotate in the app, and protein powder and shakes appear in health and wellness promotions from time to time, though we cannot predict when. The Target Circle Card takes 5 percent off most purchases at the time of writing and stacks with sale prices. Check current terms in the app before you rely on any discount.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What plant-based protein powders does Target sell?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Target stocks Orgain, OWYN, Garden of Life, Truvani and Ka'Chava among others, at the time of writing. Orgain's 2.03 lb tub is our pick on value and taste reports; OWYN suits households avoiding several allergens; Garden of Life's 20 g Organic Protein is also good value. Ka'Chava is a meal shake and costs far more per gram of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Target protein powder third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Mostly not. Of the powders we rank, only Vital Proteins Collagen Peptides is NSF Certified for Sport on the certifier's registry. Optimum Nutrition says Gold Standard is banned-substance tested. If you compete in a tested sport, check the exact product and flavor on the NSF or Informed Sport database before buying, and ask a doctor about any health concerns.
                </p>
